:: R2-Scene5-Bedroom [release2]
Late. Midnight or close to it. The apartment has that charged quiet of a space where something just changed and the furniture hasn't caught up yet.

Jess is in bed. But not sleeping. She's on her phone, screen held close to her face, scrolling with her thumb. She's pulled up Marcus's social analytics on some tool I don't recognize. Growth charts and engagement rates and audience demographics spread across her screen like a patient's vitals.

She's animated. Muttering to herself. Making little sounds of discovery. //"Oh wow."// //"That's smart."// //"He's not even monetizing this properly."//

I stand in the doorway and watch her. The ambitious, sharp, fire in the belly Jess who I fell for three years ago at a college networking event where she was the only person who actually knew what a conversion funnel was. She had a notepad. An actual notepad, paper, with color coded sections. I asked if I could buy her a coffee and she said I could buy her a coffee if I could tell her our channel's engagement rate without looking it up. I couldn't. She let me buy the coffee anyway.

That Jess. She's back. The problem is she's back for someone else.

//"I did the math,"// she says without looking up. //"If I manage him at even a basic rate, that's three times what I make at the store. And if he hits a million? Ethan, we could move. Get a real apartment. You could go full time streaming without worrying about rent."//

She looks up. Her eyes have that brightness. The data brightness and the other brightness layered together. She believes every word she's saying. The part about us. About the apartment. About my career.

She's framing this as being about us and right now, tonight, she's right. It is about us. She hasn't lied yet. She hasn't even considered the possibility that she'll need to. This is Jess Ohara at her most sincere, sitting in a bed with sheets that need washing in an apartment that smells like leftover ramen, believing that she can walk into a man's orbit and come home with nothing but a paycheck.

I look at her and I love her and something in my chest tightens like a hand closing around an egg.

